          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong group
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+structure"} algebraic structure
            consisting of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+operation"} binary operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star
            that satisfies the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong group axioms
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"}> associativity
              \:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall a,b,c \in G: (a \star b) \star c = a \star (b \star c)
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity"}> identity
              \:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi e
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %mi e
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists e \in G, \forall a \in G: e \star a = a \star e = a
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"}> inverse
              \:
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ∃
                    %msup
                      %mi a
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋆
                    %msup
                      %mi a
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi a
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall a \in G, \exists a^{-1} \in G: a  \star  a^{-1} = a^{-1} \star a = e
          %p
            It follows that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a^{-1}
            is unique for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is non-empty.
          %p
            In a broader sense, a group is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            in which every element has a (necessarily unique)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+element"} inverse
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+objects"} group objects
            (see
            %em
              %a{:href => "#Internalization"} Internalization
            below), one should rather say that a group is a monoid together with an inversion operation.
          %p
            An
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            is a group where the order in which two elements are multiplied is irrelevant. That is, it satisfies
            = succeed ":" do
              %em commutativity
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall a,b \in G : a \star b = b \star a
          %h2#delooping Delooping
          %p
            To some extent, a group “is” a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            with a single object, or more precisely a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed
            groupoid with a single object.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            of a group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B} G
            with
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Obj
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Obj(\mathbf{B}G) = \{\bullet\}
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Hom
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo •
                      %mo ,
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_{\mathbf{B}G}(\bullet, \bullet) = G
                \.
          %p
            Since for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ,
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G, H
            two groups,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G \to \mathbf{B}H
            are canonically in bijection with group homomorphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \to H
          %p
            Let
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
            be the 1-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            whose objects are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            and whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            (discarding the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            be the category of groups. Then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Grp
                  %mo →
                  %mi Grpd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B} \colon Grp \to Grpd
          %p
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
            of groupoids on groupoids with a single object.
          %p It is in this sense that a group really is a groupoid with a single object.
          %p
            But notice that it is unnatural to think of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
            as a 1-category. It is really a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            on groupoids.
          %p
            And the category of groups is
            %em not
            equivalent to the full sub-2-category of the 2-category of groupoids on one-object groupoids.
          %p The reason is that two functors:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B}f_1, \mathbf{B}f_2 \colon \mathbf{B}G \to \mathbf{B}H
          %p
            coming from two group homomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1, f_2 \colon G \to H
            are related by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mi h
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_h \colon \mathbf{B}f_1 \to \mathbf{B}f_2
            with single component
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mi h
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo •
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Mor
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_h \colon \bullet \mapsto h \in Mor(\mathbf{B} H)
            for each element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \in H
            such that the homomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_1
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_2
            differ by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+automorphism"} inner automorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ad
                    %mi h
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi H
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ad_h \colon H \to H
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mi h
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Ad
                    %mi h
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\eta_h \colon \mathbf{B}f_1 \to \mathbf{B}f_2)
                  \\Leftrightarrow
                  (f_2 = Ad_h \circ f_1)
                  \\,.
          %p
            To fix this, look at the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+object"} pointed
            groupoids with
            %span.newWikiWord
              pointed functors
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/pointed+functor"} ?
            and pointed natural transformations. Between group homomorphisms as above, only identity transformations are pointed, so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Grp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grp
            becomes a full sub-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            \-category of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Grpd
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Grpd_*
            (one that happens to be a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/1-category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lectures+on+n-Categories+and+Cohomology"} Lectures on n-Categories and Cohomology
            and should probably be added to
            %span.newWikiWord
              pointed functor
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/pointed+functor"} ?
            and maybe also
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-tuply+monoidal+n-category"} k-tuply monoidal n-category
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %h3#Internalization Internalization
          %p
            A
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
            (see there for more)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal to
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
            (hence in particular with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            together with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi mult
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi G
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} mult \colon G \times G\to G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi id
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id \colon \ast \to G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi inv
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} inv \colon G\to G
            such that various diagrams commute expressing associativity, unitality, and inverses (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "group+object#InCartesianMonoidalCategory"} there
          %p
            Equivalently, it is a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Grp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}\to Grp
            whose underlying functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op} \to Set
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
          %p
            For example, a group object in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diff"} Diff
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+group"} Lie group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+group"} topological group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sch%2FS"} Sch/S
            (the category or
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+schemes"} relative schemes
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+scheme"} group scheme
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi CAlg
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CAlg^{op}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CAlg"} CAlg
            is the category of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebras"} commutative algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
          %p
            A group object in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            is the same thing as an abelian group (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eckmann-Hilton+argument"} Eckmann-Hilton argument
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            is the same thing as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal category
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+module"} crossed module
          %h3#in_higher_categorical_and_homotopical_contexts In higher categorical and homotopical contexts
          %p
            Internalizing the notion of
            %em group
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopical
            contexts yields various generalized notions. For instance
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-group"} 2-group
                is a group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grpd"} Grpd
            %li
              %p
                an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-group"} n-group
                is a group object internal to
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-groupoid"} n-groupoid
            %li
              %p
                an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group"} ∞-group
                is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object+in+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} group object in an (∞,1)-category
            %li
              %p
                a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
                is a group object in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            %li
              %p
                generally there is a notion of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} group object in an (infinity,1)-category
          %p
            And the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+object"} loop space object
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            makes sense (at least) in any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (infinity,1)-category
          %p
            Notice that the relation between group objects and deloopable objects becomes more subtle as one generalizes this way. For instance not every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid+object+in+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} group object in an (infinity,1)-category
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} deloopable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"} (infinity,1)-topos
            is.
          %h3#weakened_axioms Weakened axioms
          %p
            Following the practice of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/centipede+mathematics"} centipede mathematics
          %ul
            %li
              remove inverses to get
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semigroups"} semigroups
            %li
              or remove associativity to get
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+%28algebra%29"} loops
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasigroups"} quasigroups
            %li
              or remove all of the above to get
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magma"} magmas
            %li
              or instead allow (in a certain way) for the binary operation to be partial to get
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semicategory"} semicategories
            %li etc.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#special_types_and_classes Special types and classes
          %ul
            %li
